1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:03,307
Some jobs belong on a schedule:

2
00:00:03,307 --> 00:00:08,108
a reminder every Monday, a cleanup every night, a report every morning.

3
00:00:08,108 --> 00:00:11,973
The Timer Event runs a workflow at the times you choose.

4
00:00:11,973 --> 00:00:17,460
In this video, you enable the timer, build a scheduled workflow, and watch it run.

5
00:00:22,060 --> 00:00:27,337
A workflow usually starts when something happens, such as a content item being published.

6
00:00:27,337 --> 00:00:30,004
The Timer Event makes the clock the event:

7
00:00:30,004 --> 00:00:33,170
the workflow runs each time its schedule comes due.

8
00:00:33,920 --> 00:00:38,031
Placed in the middle of a workflow, the Timer Event instead makes the

9
00:00:38,031 --> 00:00:42,691
workflow wait until the next time its schedule matches, and then continues with

10
00:00:42,691 --> 00:00:44,500
the activities that follow.

11
00:00:45,250 --> 00:00:50,179
The schedule is a CRON expression, written as five fields separated by spaces:

12
00:00:50,179 --> 00:00:54,170
the minute, the hour, the day of the month, the month, and the

13
00:00:54,170 --> 00:00:55,461
day of the week.

14
00:00:55,461 --> 00:00:58,160
A star matches every value of its field.

15
00:00:58,910 --> 00:01:00,808
Here are four examples:

16
00:01:00,808 --> 00:01:05,912
every five minutes, which is the default; every hour, on the hour; every

17
00:01:05,912 --> 00:01:09,970
day at 8:00; and on the first day of each month at 9:00.

18
00:01:14,174 --> 00:01:17,293
The Timer Event comes from a feature of its own.

19
00:01:17,293 --> 00:01:20,124
Open Tools, Features, and search for Timer.

20
00:01:21,095 --> 00:01:26,779
Timer Workflows Activities, in the Workflows category, needs the Workflows feature, which this

21
00:01:26,779 --> 00:01:28,541
site already has enabled.

22
00:01:28,541 --> 00:01:32,975
Click Enable, and the Timer Event is available in the workflow designer.

23
00:01:37,200 --> 00:01:39,685
In the Design menu, open Workflows.

24
00:01:39,685 --> 00:01:43,080
The list is empty, so let's click Create Workflow.

25
00:01:43,533 --> 00:01:45,394
A workflow only needs a name.

26
00:01:45,394 --> 00:01:49,753
Let's call this one Scheduled check, and click Create and open the designer.

27
00:01:51,869 --> 00:01:55,332
An empty workflow offers common events to start it with.

28
00:01:55,332 --> 00:02:00,249
A workflow that runs on a schedule starts with the Timer Event, so let's click it.

29
00:02:00,722 --> 00:02:05,910
The Timer Event is marked Start, and its settings open in a panel on the canvas.

30
00:02:05,910 --> 00:02:10,134
The CRON Expression field holds the default, every five minutes.

31
00:02:10,134 --> 00:02:14,116
For this demonstration, type five stars separated by spaces:

32
00:02:14,116 --> 00:02:15,262
every minute.

33
00:02:15,740 --> 00:02:18,342
The expression is read in UTC by default.

34
00:02:18,342 --> 00:02:22,770
Check Use site time zone to read it in the time zone of your site instead.

35
00:02:23,223 --> 00:02:27,027
Click the Done outcome of the Timer Event, and choose Log Task.

36
00:02:27,027 --> 00:02:30,445
The Log Task writes a line to the log of the site, which

37
00:02:30,445 --> 00:02:32,153
keeps this example small.

38
00:02:32,605 --> 00:02:35,160
In Text, enter the scheduled check ran.

39
00:02:35,160 --> 00:02:38,965
The designer saves your changes as you make them, in a draft.

40
00:02:39,434 --> 00:02:40,933
A draft doesn't run.

41
00:02:40,933 --> 00:02:44,333
Click Publish, and the workflow runs on its schedule.

42
00:02:44,333 --> 00:02:46,754
Each run is recorded as an instance.

43
00:02:50,929 --> 00:02:54,815
The site checks its timers once a minute, so an instance appears within

44
00:02:54,815 --> 00:02:56,582
about a minute of publishing.

45
00:02:56,582 --> 00:02:58,903
Click Instances next to Scheduled check:

46
00:02:58,903 --> 00:03:02,739
one instance is Halted, because it waits for the schedule to come due.

47
00:03:03,213 --> 00:03:08,016
Each time the schedule comes due, the waiting instance continues through the Log

48
00:03:08,016 --> 00:03:11,647
Task and is Finished, and the next instance starts waiting.

49
00:03:11,647 --> 00:03:14,663
The Finished count goes up by one for every run.

50
00:03:15,121 --> 00:03:16,979
Open a finished instance.

51
00:03:16,979 --> 00:03:22,851
Its page highlights the Timer Event and the Log Task, the activities that this run executed.

52
00:03:27,051 --> 00:03:31,284
A background task named Timed Workflow Starter does the checking, so a timer

53
00:03:31,284 --> 00:03:33,865
can run up to about a minute after its time.

54
00:03:33,865 --> 00:03:36,601
A schedule finer than a minute is not possible.

55
00:03:37,351 --> 00:03:39,847
Timers work only while the site runs.

56
00:03:39,847 --> 00:03:45,011
By default, after a restart they resume once the site has served its first request.

57
00:03:45,761 --> 00:03:49,509
A timer that fires often leaves one instance for each run.

58
00:03:49,509 --> 00:03:55,891
To keep the list short, check Delete successfully completed workflows on the Workflow tab, and publish.

59
00:03:57,891 --> 00:04:02,023
The Timer Event turns a CRON expression into a schedule for a workflow,

60
00:04:02,023 --> 00:04:04,276
or into a wait in the middle of one.

61
00:04:04,276 --> 00:04:05,671
Thanks for watching.

